    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> began the Angkorian period around 800 CE, and Yasovarman built the city of Yasodharapura around the hill of Phnom Bakheng from 889.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> between 1113 and 1150, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> sack of 1177 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> built the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> over the ruins of the old capital.</li><li>Angkor was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> around 1431 and nearly all of it was abandoned in the 15th century, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> remained a Buddhist shrine.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A study in 2007 using satellite photographs concluded that Angkor had been the largest pre-industrial city in the world by surface area, with an urban sprawl of at least 1,000 square kilometres.</p></aside>
    </section>

