    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">802</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> declares himself universal monarch and god-king</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">889</span><span class="rf-text">Yasovarman ascends the throne and builds Yasodharapura around Phnom Bakheng</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1113</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> begins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, which is built between 1113 and 1150</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1177</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> forces sack the capital and kill the reigning king</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1181</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> takes the throne and builds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1296</span><span class="rf-text">The Chinese diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> arrives at Angkor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1431</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> sacks Angkor and its population moves south to Longvek</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1907</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient">École française d'Extrême-Orient</a> begins the restoration of the site</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ruins of Angkor stand amid forests and farmland north of the Great Lake (Tonlé Sap) and south of the Kulen Hills, near the present city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siem-reap">Siem Reap</a>. Archaeologists dated the shift at Angkor from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> to the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> to 420 BCE, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> of bivalve shells found in graves. The earliest Iron Age finds were iron and bronze spearheads, tool kits with iron-bladed knives, hoes for tilling land and bangles. Before the Angkorian period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> consisted of a number of politically independent principalities, known to the Chinese as Funan and Chenla.</p><p>The Angkorian period began shortly after 800 CE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> announced the independence of Kambujadesa (Cambodia) from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. According to the Sdok Kok Thom inscription, his first capital, around 781, was Indrapura, near today's Kompong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>. He became king of Kambuja in 790, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-kulen">Mahendraparvata</a> in the Kulen mountains and founded Hariharalaya, now known as Roluos. In 802 he declared himself a universal monarch (chakravartin) and a god-king (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a>). Yasovarman ascended in 889 and built a new city, Yasodharapura, near Hariharalaya. It was centred on the hill and temple of Phnom Bakheng, and he also built a large reservoir called a baray. The name Angkor derives from the Khmer nokor, "kingdom", which comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> nagara, "city".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="Angkor Wat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between 900 and 1200 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> produced the architectural works known as Angkor. Most stand within an area about 24 km from east to west and 8 km from north to south, and around 72 major temples or other buildings lie there, with the remains of several hundred minor temple sites scattered beyond. Because medieval Khmer settlement was low in density and dispersed, Angkor had no formal boundary, but roads and canals defined an area of at least 1,000 square kilometres around the major temples, and in spatial extent, though not in population, it was the largest urban agglomeration in recorded history before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>.</p><p>Angkor has been called a "hydraulic city", because a water management network stabilised, stored and dispersed water across the area. This network is believed to have been used for irrigation, to offset the unpredictable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and to support the growing population. Scholars have debated whether the barays served to irrigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields or were symbols of the oceans around Mount Meru. The size of the population remains a subject of research and debate; newly identified agricultural systems may have supported between 750,000 and 1,000,000 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> between 1113 and 1150, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>, called the greatest of the Angkorian kings, oversaw the most prolific period of construction, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preah-khan">Preah Khan</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pre-rup"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pre-rup.jpg" alt="Pre Rup" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pre-rup">Pre Rup</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pre_Rup,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-16,_DD_13.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Angkor was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, and its kings followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> in claiming the status of a god-king. An inscription dated 881 records that Indravarman I erected a lingam named Indresvara. Yasovarman ascended in 889 and built Yasodharapura, and Rajendravarman, whose reign began in 944, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pre-rup">Pre Rup</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> came to the throne after defeating a rival prince in battle; an inscription says that he leapt onto his rival's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephant</a> and killed him. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> as his personal temple mausoleum, and after his death around 1150 the kingdom fell into internal strife.</p><p>In 1177 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, the neighbours of the Khmer to the east, invaded by water, sacked Yasodharapura and killed the reigning king. A Khmer prince rallied his people and defeated the Cham, and in 1181 he took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>. He adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> as his personal faith and made it the state religion. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> restoration followed, beginning around 1243 with the death of his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indravarman-ii">Indravarman II</a>. In 1296 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> arrived at Angkor during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indravarman-iii">Indravarman III</a>. The Angkorian period lasted until the late 14th century, and Angkor first fell under Ayutthayan suzerainty in 1351. After the sack of 1431 the official capital later moved to Oudong in 1618 and to the present site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> in 1865.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Angkor&#8221; cites where it covers Seat of the Khmer Empire, End of the Angkorian period:</p><ul><li>David Chandler, A History of Cambodia (Westview Press, 1992)</li><li>Charles Higham, The Civilization of Angkor (University of California Press / London, 2001)</li><li>Charles Higham, The Archaeology of Mainland Southeast Asia (Cambridge University Press, 1989)</li><li>George Coedès, The Indianized states of Southeast Asia (University of Hawaii Press, 1968)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ta-prohm.jpg" alt="Ta Prohm" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ta_Prohm_(III).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ..., CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Angkor was a religious centre and also a city that served all the needs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> people. All the remaining monuments except a few old bridges are religious buildings, because in Angkorian times all other buildings, including the king's residence, were built of perishable materials such as wood, "because only the gods had a right to residences made of stone". Most surviving inscriptions concern religious foundations, so the history of the state religion is easier to write than that of other parts of society. Roads and canals intertwined through the city in a grid-like pattern around the buildings, and the canals supported irrigation. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> a stone carving states that 12,640 people serviced that one temple complex, and about 300,000 agricultural workers have been estimated as necessary to feed the workers of the temples.</p><p>The Chinese diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> stayed at Angkor for a year from 1296 and wrote The Customs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, about forty pages on religion, justice, kingship, agriculture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, clothing, tools and commerce. He described a royal procession of soldiers, servant women and concubines, ministers and princes, and finally the sovereign, "standing on an elephant, holding his sacred sword in his hand". He found three religious groups: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist monks with shaven heads and yellow robes, Brahmans, who were often high officials, and Shaivites, whom he called "Taoists". The bas-reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> show the king's court, battles with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> and village life.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Angkor&#8221; cites where it covers Religious history, Pre-Angkorian religion, Shiva and the lingam, Vaishnavism:</p><ul><li>Charles Higham, The Civilization of Angkor (University of California Press / London, 2001)</li><li>David Chandler, A History of Cambodia (Westview Press, 1992)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bayon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayon.jpg" alt="Bayon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayon,_Angkor_Thom,_Camboya,_2013-08-17,_DD_37.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> invasion of 1177 sacked Yasodharapura and killed the king. Religious change brought its own damage. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples were altered to display images of the Buddha, and after his death the revival of Hinduism included a large-scale campaign of desecrating Buddhist images. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-viii">Jayavarman VIII</a>, a Shaivite iconoclast, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> was made a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and its central Buddha statue, 3.6 metres tall, was cast to the bottom of a nearby well.</p><p>The city had already been in decline in the 13th and 14th centuries. Ongoing civil wars with the Lavo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and the Suphannaphum-Mon dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> were sapping Angkor's strength by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a>, who reported that the country had been completely devastated by such a war. After the Ayutthayan sack of around 1431 many statues were taken to Ayutthaya, and others went to the new centre of Khmer society at Longvek. Paleobotanical and stratigraphic evidence shows that the moat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> was no longer in use around that time. The main cause of the decline is unknown, and scholars have suggested the Ayutthaya invasions, the erosion of the royal cult under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, neglect of the waterways, disease, earthquakes, floods, and drought in the early 15th century, as tree-ring records show. During the 15th century nearly all of Angkor was abandoned, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, which remained a Buddhist shrine.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Angkor never ceased to be known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>. Practice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> at Angkor continues to this day, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> was not completely abandoned in the 17th century: fourteen inscriptions of that century record Japanese settlements beside those of the remaining Khmer, and the best known tells of Ukondafu Kazufusa, who celebrated the Khmer New Year there in 1632. The Portuguese friar António da Madalena was the first recorded European visitor to Angkor Wat, in 1586. Henri Mouhot visited in 1860 and Anna Leonowens in 1865, and the archaeologist Louis Delaporte and the ethnologist Adolf Bastian then visited and made the site known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>From 1907 to 1970 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient">École française d'Extrême-Orient</a> directed work at the site, clearing the forest, repairing foundations and installing drains. Scholars of the school, among them George Coedès, Maurice Glaize, Paul Mus and Philippe Stern, began the historical scholarship on which the present understanding of Angkor rests. Work resumed after the Cambodian Civil War, and since 1993 it has been co-ordinated by an international committee, while the Cambodian agency APSARA, created in 1995, carries out the Cambodian work. Some temples have been taken apart stone by stone and reassembled on concrete foundations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared Angkor a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1992. In 2007 a team using satellite photographs and other techniques mapped the extent of its water network. Angkor survives as ruins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siem-reap">Siem Reap</a> Province.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Angkor was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, begun as Yasodharapura under Yasovarman in 889 and spread over at least 1,000 square kilometres with temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and canals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> were built there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> sacked the city around 1431.</em></p>

